#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61b2cbe-e283-5d14-b24c-72dded057638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+\\,j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398328} {:answer/id #uuid "843c6fdf-9f38-56ac-8525-881bcff219b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398329} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b83354-a308-50ea-9ee4-85aaf20726b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398330} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1c6878-8ab0-51e2-8005-62b32908bc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398331} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7cd7b0-03c7-513f-a03d-32eb59e6232b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i-2\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5b83354-a308-50ea-9ee4-85aaf20726b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398330} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a28c00-4228-59a1-bd55-b03951c2d0fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d57e43b-681d-5aa1-a53c-acdd85847d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-127600" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c0bb344-e2e7-53b7-a86b-a70876c813ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483884} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d3585e-134e-53e5-b3ff-d54d2085a716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd425a39-318f-5566-a2e2-b27e6f8eab87" :question/math-academy-id "q-223098" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d617821-6685-519e-9a4d-28281ac958a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288333} {:answer/id #uuid "099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "099d7c1b-7772-5cc6-9777-a7e269acac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483587} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ee93b5a-7a7f-5de6-b120-52987f2cfbac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "705b4f99-555b-54e9-8531-2285040b4156" :question/math-academy-id "q-223101" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$k$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k)}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a35ec37-2a33-551d-8e81-252c71bec628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483673} :answer-field/id #uuid "83efe503-c6c7-5398-8b5a-e3c31636860c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34386db1-c26a-52c0-8482-81ad51998468" :question/math-academy-id "q-358781" :question/problem "$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k).\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{5}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-4\\,k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}‖\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)}{\\frac{3}{5}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "375ef518-4d6a-56b5-8701-cd9802686411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342695} {:answer/id #uuid "41e5f4d8-856b-52f5-85b3-07ac865cef2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342784} {:answer/id #uuid "69d5fca1-eec8-5d54-b1fc-d0f9751e2efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343141} {:answer/id #uuid "745b8d3b-d175-5c42-a346-9db9b829c2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343245} {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1ce442-73ac-5780-9670-756b226a0a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343779} :answer-field/id #uuid "74402250-c07b-5643-a659-e21ce9c35c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15f4c73-c376-5fbd-b671-915cb2fd0f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75491" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
